      "en": "I would note on the one hand that this world is crammed with problems, and on the other that it is animated by the will to leave no room for mystery. I reserve the right to define more technically in a moment the meaning of this distinction—which to my mind is fundamental—between problem and mystery. For the present, I shall confine myself to pointing out that to eliminate or attempt to eliminate mystery is, in the functionalized world of which we have spoken, to bring into play, in the presence of events that break the routine of existence—birth, love, death—that psychological and pseudo-scientific category of the 'entirely natural' (le tout naturel) which would deserve a separate study. This is, in truth, only the residue of a degraded rationalism for which the cause explains the effect, that is to say, accounts for it entirely. In a world of this sort there is, by contrast, an infinity of problems, because the causes are not known to us in detail, and there is therefore room for indefinite investigation. Outside these theoretical problems, there are also innumerable technical problems connected with the question of how the various vital and social functions, having been labeled and inventoried, can be exercised without detriment to one another. Between theoretical problems and technical problems there is, moreover, a rigorous solidarity; for theoretical problems depend on definite techniques, and conversely technical problems can be solved only where a certain theoretical knowledge has previously been established."
