      "en": "A similar exigence, let us observe, is already at the heart of the most radical pessimism. Pessimism has meaning only if it signifies: being ought assuredly to be; yet being is not—and I myself who make this observation am consequently nothing. As for defining more precisely what this word 'being' means, let us agree that it is extremely difficult. I would propose only this avenue of approach: being is what resists—or would be what resisted—an exhaustive analysis bearing upon the data of experience and attempting to reduce them step by step to elements more and more devoid of intrinsic or significative value (it is an analysis of this order that is pursued through the theoretical works of Freud, for instance)."
