      "en": "A philosophy that refuses to take the ontological exigence into account is, however, possible; and it is even toward this abstention that modern thought as a whole has drifted. But here we must distinguish two attitudes which one might sometimes be tempted to confuse: the first consists in maintaining oneself systematically in reserve—this will be the agnostic attitude under all its forms; the other, much bolder, more courageous, more coherent, tends to see in the ontological exigence the expression of an obsolete dogmatism which idealist criticism has disposed of once and for all."
